{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["The reason for <b>\"and they brought their offering, one goat for HaShem\"</b> — [this refers] to the bull of the burnt offering mentioned above; <b>\"and their sin\"</b> [refers] to the goat of the sin offering. He mentioned it thus for the greatness of the sin, which extends to all the commandments of HaShem, to say that even though the sin is exceedingly great, since it is unintentional and they brought upon it their offering, a burnt offering and a sin offering, it is fitting to atone for them. And in the Sifrei (Shelach 111) they expounded the verse for its addition: Rabbi Yoshiyah says — any tribe that acted upon a ruling of its court and the other tribes did likewise, whence do we know that they would bring [an offering] through them? Scripture says: \"and they brought their offering, one goat for HaShem.\" Rabbi Yonatan says, etc. And by way of truth, I have already made known to you that every place where 'HaShem' is said is in the attribute of mercy, and 'before HaShem' is like 'before Me,' and this is as they said: 'He and his court.' Now he said that they brought their offering to the singular Name and their sin before Me, and behold the sin is atoned through the attribute of mercy and the attribute of justice. He mentioned this in the context of idolatry, but in the book of Leviticus, regarding the other commandments, he mentioned many times 'before HaShem,' and this is explained."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The speaker of 'He mentioned' (ה״זכיר) throughout is Scripture/Moses; Ramban refers to the repetend in the verse as the basis for both halakhic derashot and the kabbalistic reading."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:05:50.809512204Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}